pproved`, `great`, `looks good`, `perfect` and others; `gut`, `passt`, `ok`, `super`, `einverstanden`, `sieht gut aus` and others; PO decision 2026-11-17, the signature before a push stays the final gate; a doubt, a negation or a "but" is never on it). If the human answers with one of those forms (case, commas, spaces and a closing `.` or `!` do not matter), rerun the identical command with `--human-acceptance "<the human's literal answer>"`. `--human-acceptance` records an acceptance for those forms only: any other answer (a bug report, a change request, a doubt, a "yes, but ...") stops with `ONESHOT-ACCEPTANCE-NOT-YES`, records nothing and names both ways on; never search the answer for a keyword and never turn it into a yes yourself. **A decline is not the end of the feature:** rerun the identical command with `--human-decline "<the human's literal answer>"` (exclusive with `--human-acceptance`). The close records the human's words with the candidate and the time in `specs/<feature>/evidence/human-decline-<candidate>-<answer>.md` (never read as an acceptance), retires the bound Result (the State transition `continuity-result-retire-plan` / `-apply`: `continuity.authority.result` becomes null, the retired binding stays in `continuity.resultRetirements`, `result.md` is restored to the text before the bootstrap block, and the package is reconciled result-only, without a signature; in a project with a session-authority catalogue this runs on the owner route like the Result bootstrap, on the Close lifecycle the one-shot already opened) and returns status `declined` with `next` in order: the commit of exactly those outputs, a correction dispatch (`prepare-dispatch.mjs goldfish`, the human's words are the correction request), a Critic delta round on the new candidate (`prepare-dispatch.mjs critic --delta-request`), then the identical close command with `--critic-packet-id <new-packet-id>`. The decline runs no Verify of its own: it first discards the uncommitted outputs 